```
ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: yellow
REAL_USE_CASE: Multi-agent LLM systems with inter-agent messaging where receiver role+task is highly predictive of act-on spans (AUC>=0.65) AND message verbosity is low-moderate (verb_frac <=0.4). This is a narrow regime; at high verbosity (verb_frac=0.8) the generic compressor wins.
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Microbenchmark-only for the general claim. The L0 shows role-conditioning helps only in bounded regime (21/36 cells) and LOSES in high-verbosity regime (12/36 cells, -0.053 at verb=0.8) where the claim predicted biggest win. The mechanism is falsified: act-on content is high-salience, so generic captures it. Real action items are likely high-salience, favoring generic.
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [Requires measuring real act-on↔salience correlation (unknown; if high, generic wins); Requires building/maintaining role-conditioned extractor (classifier/LLM-judge) vs using off-the-shelf LLMLingua; Need to measure role-AUC in production traffic (if <0.65, generic wins); Win is conditional on low-moderate verbosity, but production MAS traffic may be high-verbosity; Generic compressor already exists, simpler, no role taxonomy needed]
RATIONALE: EXP-0023 shows rolecond beats generic only when AUC>=0.65 AND verb_frac low-moderate; at verb=0.8 generic wins +0.053 (CI excludes 0). The claim's narrative ("verbose messages have role-irrelevant content only a role filter can drop") is NOT what the sim shows: "under a realistic model where act-on content is also high-salience, a generic salience filter captures act-on content well" (packet). Prior art: LLMLingua/LongLLMLingua are published role-agnostic compressors; MAS comms-efficiency is active (CodeAct, etc.). The narrow novelty's advantage is "CONDITIONAL not general per this L0". L1 would measure real act-on↔salience coupling; "if real act-on content is high-salience (likely for action items), generic may already capture most of the win" (packet).
```
